#
# The same one symbol samples/modelblaster_pext declares, for the same reason: pext.h
# reads CONFIG_MB_PEXT directly (Zephyr puts autoconf.h in front of every translation
# unit), so declaring it here is all that is needed to make the header emit real
# encodings in this app.
#
# It is NOT shared with that sample deliberately. A Kconfig fragment sourced by two apps
# would be one more thing that has to be in scope for either of them to build; one
# symbol, copied, with the same help text, costs less than that.

mainmenu "TACIT on two harts, with MBP on one of them"

config MB_PEXT
	bool "Build the MBP body against the real extension"
	default y
	help
	  Emit the real MBP.DOT8 / MBP.MAX8 / MBP.QMUL / MBP.CLIP8 encodings
	  (custom-0, opcode 0x0b) for the body hart 0 runs.

	  THE EXTENSION EXISTS ON HART 0 ONLY.  The body hart 1 runs calls
	  pext.h's `_sw` software model explicitly and is unaffected by this
	  symbol, which is what lets one image carry both instruction mixes.

	  With this off, hart 0 would execute the software model too, both
	  traces would contain the same instructions, and the sample refuses
	  to run rather than reporting a heterogeneous trace that is not one.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
